// AUTO-GENERATED — Soul Game Cinematic Data V2
const ACT1 = [

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 0: THE WAKING (Shared Opening)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    {
        id: 1,
        ch: "The Waking",
        set: "Your eyes open. You are sitting in a cold metal chair bolted to a concrete floor. The room is small — maybe four meters square — and blindingly white. Fluorescent tubes buzz overhead like a dying nervous system. Your wrists are raw and red; the leather restraints that held you hang loose from the armrests, sliced clean through, though you have no memory of cutting them. The air tastes of disinfectant and copper. On your left forearm, tattooed in black ink, is a number: 7742. You do not know your name.",
        act: "A red alarm light begins strobing. The floor vibrates beneath your bare feet. Three features of the room demand your attention: to your LEFT, a square metal hatch in the floor — cold air rises from it and you can hear the distant rumble of machinery far below. AHEAD of you, the entire wall is a one-way mirror — your own gaunt reflection stares back, but behind the glass, shadows are moving. To your RIGHT, a reinforced steel door with a keypad — the lock light flickers between red and green, sparking softly.",
        dlg: "A synthetic voice fills the room from hidden speakers, calm and precise: 'Subject 7742. Sedation protocol complete. Orientation phase initiated. Select an exit vector. Non-compliance will result in permanent archival. You have thirty seconds.'",
        c: [
            ["Pull open the floor hatch and drop into the dark", "You grab the hatch with both hands and wrench it upward. Below is nothing — just cold black air and the distant echo of dripping water. You don't think. You sit on the edge, take a breath, and push yourself off. You fall for two terrifying seconds before slamming onto a metal grate. The hatch crashes shut above you. The alarm becomes a muffled drone. You're in total darkness. The air is damp and smells of rust and grease. You feel the walls — narrow, barely wider than your shoulders. A maintenance tunnel. Going down.", "N", 3, 10],
            ["Smash through the one-way mirror", "You stand up from the chair, grab it by the legs, and swing it into the mirror with everything you have. The glass explodes inward, raining shards into the room behind it. You climb through the jagged frame, cutting your palms, and drop onto a clean tile floor. You're in a small, dark observation room. Six monitors glow on a desk, each showing a different angle of the white room you just left. A half-eaten sandwich sits next to a cold cup of coffee. Someone was here moments ago. A white lab coat hangs on the back of a rolling chair. A door at the back of the room is still swinging — whoever was watching you just ran.", "E", 3, 20],
            ["Punch the sparking keypad until the door opens", "You slam your fist into the keypad. Once. Twice. On the third hit, the sparking light turns green and the door slides open with a pneumatic hiss. Beyond it: a long corridor lit by red emergency strips along the floor. The walls are reinforced concrete. Security cameras track your movement as you step through the threshold. Behind you, the door seals shut with a definitive click. Ahead, at the far end of the corridor, you hear the mechanical whirr of something powering up — a shape rounds the corner. A drone, hovering at chest height, with a crackling stun-prod extended like a sword.", "C", 3, 30]
        ]
    },

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 1: THE ENVIRONMENT (3 scenes)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 10: THE DROP (from floor hatch) ---
    {
        id: 10,
        ch: "The Drop",
        set: "You are lying on a metal grate in a narrow maintenance tunnel. The grate is wet. Above you, the sealed hatch is a faint square of light that fades as your eyes adjust. The tunnel is barely a meter wide, lined with bundled electrical cables and leaking pipes. The air is thick with humidity and the smell of old grease. The tunnel stretches in both directions — to your left, dim yellow emergency lights flicker every few meters; to your right, pure darkness, but you can feel a warm breeze on your face, carrying the faint stench of chemicals.",
        act: "Behind you, from the direction of the hatch, you hear a metallic CLANG. Then another. Something is following you down. You don't have time to think — you need to move. The left path has light but is narrower. The right path is wide enough to run but you can't see a thing. Straight ahead, if you look closely, there's a side-panel in the wall that's been pried open — someone else was here before you.",
        dlg: "From far above, muffled through layers of metal, the synthetic voice echoes: 'Subject 7742 has deviated from primary containment. Retrieval protocol initiated. Estimated capture window: four minutes.'",
        c: [
            ["Sprint left toward the emergency lights", "You scramble to your feet and run toward the flickering yellow lights. The tunnel narrows. Your shoulders scrape the walls. The cables catch on your jumpsuit. After thirty seconds of blind running, the floor drops away — you stumble into a junction where three massive steam pipes cross the tunnel like iron arteries. Steam hisses from a cracked valve, filling the passage with scalding white vapor. You can't go back — the clanging is closer now.", "N", 5, 11],
            ["Stop moving. Press against the wall. Listen.", "You freeze. You press your back against the cold metal and hold your breath. The clanging behind you slows... then stops. In the silence that follows, you hear something else: a voice. Faint. Human. Coming from somewhere below you, through the grate beneath your feet. It's whispering your number. '7742... 7742... down here...' You look down through the grate. Fifteen feet below, in a lower tunnel, you see the faint glow of a face looking up at you.", "A", 3, 12],
            ["Squeeze through the pried-open side panel", "You shove your body through the gap in the wall panel. The metal edges cut your forearm, but you pull yourself through into a cramped utility closet. And there, at the back of the closet, embedded in the concrete wall, is something that shouldn't be here: a small screen, about the size of a tablet, glowing soft blue. It shows a map — corridors, rooms, levels. A red dot pulses in the center: 'YOU ARE HERE.' Another dot, green, pulses far below, labeled: 'EXIT — SUBLEVEL 9.' Someone left this for you.", "O", 5, 13]
        ]
    },

    // --- 20: BEHIND THE GLASS (from smashing the mirror) ---
    {
        id: 20,
        ch: "Behind the Glass",
        set: "The observation room is cramped and cold. Six monitors hum on a metal desk, each showing a feed of the white room you escaped from — the shattered mirror, the empty chair, the dangling restraints. A keyboard with a cracked spacebar sits beneath the monitors. Post-it notes are stuck to the desk: 'Increase sedation for 7742?' 'Check emotional baseline — anomalous readings.' 'Prof. Mercer wants Thursday's data by 9.' The white lab coat on the chair smells of lavender. The name tag reads: 'DR. LENA VASIK — Behavioral Assessment.' The only exit is the door at the back, still swinging from whoever fled.",
        act: "You can hear footsteps retreating down a hallway beyond the door — Dr. Vasik is running. On the desk, her tablet is still unlocked, showing a half-written report: 'Subject 7742 shows unprecedented resistance to...' — the sentence is unfinished. The monitors flicker. One of them switches from your room to a different feed: a girl, maybe sixteen, sitting in an identical white room in an identical chair. She is crying. The number on her arm reads 0102.",
        dlg: "Through the door, echoing down the hall, you hear Vasik's panicked voice on a radio: 'Control, this is Lab 4 — 7742 has breached the observation wall! I need security on Level 2, NOW! ...No, I don't know how they cut the restraints — just send someone!'",
        c: [
            ["Chase Dr. Vasik — grab her before she escapes", "You vault over the desk and burst through the door into a sterile white corridor. Vasik is twenty meters ahead, white coat billowing. She glances back and her eyes go wide with terror. You sprint. She's fast, but you're faster. You catch her at a T-junction, grabbing her coat. She spins, dropping her radio. 'Don't hurt me!' she gasps, pressing her back against the wall. Her hands are trembling. She's not a soldier — she's a scientist. Up close, you see her eyes are red from crying. She's been awake for days.", "A", 1, 21],
            ["Grab her tablet and read everything before she comes back", "You snatch the tablet from the desk. Your file is open. You swipe through pages of data: heart rate logs, sleep pattern analyses, 'emotional vector mapping.' Then you find a folder labeled 'CLASSIFIED — PROJECT SOUL.' Inside is a document dated three years ago: 'Proposal: Personality-based simulation environment for involuntary subjects. Objective: complete psychological classification in under 48 hours.' There are 600 subject files. You are number 7742. You were admitted four days ago. Your admission photo shows a face you don't recognize — your own. Under 'ORIGIN,' it says one word: 'VOLUNTEER.'", "O", 5, 22],
            ["Ignore Vasik. Explore the observation room for more clues.", "You let Vasik go. Instead, you search the observation room methodically. Behind a filing cabinet, you find a second door — this one is heavy, reinforced, and labeled 'DIRECTOR — AUTHORIZED PERSONNEL ONLY.' It's locked, but the key is taped to the underside of Vasik's desk drawer. You turn it. The door opens into a private office: mahogany desk, real leather chair, a window overlooking a vast underground facility — catwalks, laboratories, hundreds of white rooms stretching into the distance like a hive. A framed photo on the desk shows a middle-aged man with two children. A plaque reads: 'Director Adrian Mercer — The Soul Project.' On his desk, a handwritten note: 'They're not volunteers. They never were. God forgive me.'", "C", 5, 23]
        ]
    },

    // --- 30: THE ALARM (from forcing the door) ---
    {
        id: 30,
        ch: "The Alarm",
        set: "A concrete corridor, ten meters long, lit by red emergency strips on the floor. The walls are bare except for painted numbers every few meters: 'SECTOR 4-A.' Security cameras pivot to track you — small black domes with red blinking lights. The air is dry and cold, recycled through vents that hum above your head. At the far end, the corridor opens into a T-junction. The emergency lights cast long, sharp shadows.",
        act: "A drone rounds the corner at the T-junction. It's the size of a basketball, hovering silently on four rotors, its single red eye locked on you. A telescoping arm extends from its underside, tipped with a crackling blue stun-prod. A second drone appears behind it. Then a third. They fan out, forming a wall of buzzing metal between you and the junction. Behind you, the sealed door clicks — the lock is recycling. There is no going back. You have maybe five seconds before they close the distance.",
        dlg: "The lead drone emits a flat, mechanical voice: 'Subject 7742. Return to containment. Non-compliance results in neurological suppression. Final warning.' The stun-prods crackle louder. The sound echoes off the concrete walls like snapping bones.",
        c: [
            ["Charge the lead drone — rip it out of the air", "You explode forward. The lead drone fires its stun-prod — it grazes your shoulder, sending a bolt of white pain down your arm — but you're already inside its guard. You grab it with both hands, feeling the rotors bite into your fingers, and slam it into the wall. It shatters in a burst of sparks and plastic. The other two drones scatter, recalibrating. In the wreckage, you see something useful: a small screen on the drone's body, cracked but readable. It shows a map of the facility. A red line traces a path: 'SECURITY CORRIDOR → ARMORY → HANGAR → ROOF.' You have a route.", "E", 5, 31],
            ["Dodge right and run — outpace them at the junction", "The stun-prod fires. You drop flat, feeling the electric bolt sizzle over your head, and roll right at the junction. The drones are fast, but they're designed for straight-line pursuit in open corridors. You take the first turn, then another, then another — each corridor identical, each number painted on the wall slightly different. 4-A gives way to 4-B, then 4-C, then 4-D. Within thirty seconds, you've lost the drones but you've also lost yourself. You're in a maze of black corridors with no emergency lights, no cameras, no sound except your own ragged breathing.", "N", 3, 32],
            ["Raise your hands. Surrender. Let them take you.", "You stop. You raise your hands above your head, palms forward. The drones hold their position, stun-prods still crackling, but they don't fire. The lead drone scans your face — a blue light passes over your features — then the mechanical voice says: 'Compliance confirmed. Subject 7742 reclassified to OBSERVATION TIER. Report to Holding Cell 7.' A door slides open to your right that you hadn't noticed — a small, white-tiled room. A cot. A sink. A window that looks out onto nothing. You step inside. The door closes behind you. But the window... the window has a hairline crack running from top to bottom. And behind the glass, you can see something glowing — pulsing — like a heartbeat.", "O", 1, 33]
        ]
    },

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 2: THE ENCOUNTER — Tunnel Branch (3 scenes)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 11: THE STEAM VENT (sprinted left in the tunnel) ---
    {
        id: 11,
        ch: "The Steam Vent",
        set: "A junction where three massive iron steam pipes — each as wide as your torso — cross the tunnel diagonally. Steam erupts from a cracked valve in rhythmic bursts, filling the passage with scalding white vapor every four seconds. Between bursts, you can see the tunnel continues on the other side, curving downward. The metal walls are dripping with condensation. The heat is suffocating. Your jumpsuit is already soaked with sweat. The clanging behind you has stopped, but you don't trust the silence.",
        act: "You need to cross through the steam. The bursts come every four seconds — you have a two-second window of clear air between each blast. Beyond the steam, the tunnel slopes downward into what sounds like a large, echoing space — you can hear water. Lots of water. To your right, a narrow maintenance hatch is bolted to the wall, rusted but not sealed. To your left, a bundle of cables runs along the ceiling — thick enough to grab and swing across, Tarzan-style.",
        dlg: "The synthetic voice, fainter now, barely audible through the layers of pipe and concrete: 'Sublevel 3 environmental hazard detected. Steam temperature: 140 degrees Celsius. Transit not recommended. Subject 7742, consider returning to primary containment for your own safety.' A pause. Then: 'Four minutes remaining.'",
        c: [
            ["Time the steam. Sprint through the gap.", "You count. One. Two. Three. BURST. One. Two — NOW. You lunge forward, ducking under the pipes. The steam erupts behind you, clipping your ankle — a flare of white-hot pain — but you're through. You tumble down the slope on the other side, sliding on wet metal, and crash into knee-deep water. You're in a massive underground chamber. The ceiling is twenty meters above you, criss-crossed with catwalks and pipes. Water is flowing in from a dozen broken valves, rising steadily. And standing on a catwalk above you, gripping the railing with white knuckles, is another person in a white jumpsuit. They're waving at you frantically.", "C", 5, 41],
            ["Grab the ceiling cables and swing across", "You leap, catch the cable bundle, and swing your body forward in an arc. The cables groan but hold. You clear the steam zone in one swing and land hard on the other side, rolling onto your shoulder. Your ankle screams from the impact but nothing is broken. The tunnel here slopes down gently, and the air cools. After twenty meters, the tunnel opens into a wide concrete chamber with a pool of still, black water at the center. On the far wall, someone has scratched words into the concrete with a sharp object: 'THEY WATCH EVERYTHING.' Beside the words, a narrow staircase leads further down into the dark.", "A", 3, 42],
            ["Open the rusted maintenance hatch instead", "You yank at the hatch. The bolts resist, then snap. Behind it is a cramped service duct — barely wide enough to crawl through — but the air coming from it is COOL. Sweet, cold, machine-room air. You squeeze yourself inside and army-crawl for what feels like an eternity — five minutes? ten? — before the duct opens into something vast. You fall out onto a metal platform overlooking an enormous room. Banks of computer servers stretch into the distance, blinking with green and amber lights. This isn't maintenance infrastructure. This is the brain of the entire facility. A terminal on the platform is logged in, cursor blinking. The last command typed was: 'INITIATE PERSONALITY EXTRACTION — ALL SUBJECTS.'", "O", 5, 43]
        ]
    },

    // --- 12: THE WHISPER (stopped and listened in the tunnel) ---
    {
        id: 12,
        ch: "The Whisper",
        set: "You are crouched in the dark tunnel, back pressed against the cold metal wall. Your breathing is controlled. The clanging above has stopped. Silence — except for one sound: a voice, human, coming from below the grate you're standing on. Fifteen feet down, in a parallel tunnel, someone is looking up at you through the mesh. Their face is dimly lit by a handheld chemical light — green, trembling. They look young. Maybe twenty. Their jumpsuit is identical to yours, but torn and filthy, smeared with what looks like engine grease. The number on their forearm reads 0014.",
        act: "Subject 14 reaches up toward the grate. 'I've been down here for two days,' they whisper. 'There's a way out — I've seen it — but I can't reach the lower tunnels alone. The water is rising down here. If you help me, I can show you the path. If you don't... I'll drown when the pumps cycle at midnight.' They point to a latch on the grate. If you open it, you can drop down to their level. But you hear the clanging again — faint, distant. The retrieval drones are still searching.",
        dlg: "'Please,' Subject 14 whispers, their voice cracking. 'My name is Sami. They took my name and gave me a number, but my name is Sami. I remember that much. I had a sister. She had a blue kite. I don't remember anything else.' They pause. 'They're draining us, 7742. Our memories, our personalities — they're extracting them. That's what this place does. That's why we can't remember who we are.'",
        c: [
            ["Open the latch. Drop down. Help Sami.", "You pull the latch and drop through the grate into the lower tunnel. Sami catches you — or tries to. You both stumble into ankle-deep water that's shockingly cold. 'Thank you,' they breathe. 'Follow me — quickly.' Sami leads you through a maze of lower tunnels, the water rising with every step. After five minutes, the tunnels open into a massive pump chamber — the source of the water. Enormous turbines churn in the dark. The water is at your waist now and rising fast. Emergency lights along the ceiling flash red. Sami points to a ladder on the far wall. 'Up there — there's a passage to the surface. But we need to swim to reach it.'", "A", 5, 41],
            ["Ask Sami about the 'extraction' — what do they mean?", "You don't open the latch. Not yet. 'What do you mean, extracting?' you whisper through the grate. Sami's face tightens. 'Every test, every room, every choice — they're mapping us. Building a copy of who we are inside the machine. When they have enough data, they don't need the original anymore.' They reach into their jumpsuit and pull out a crumpled piece of paper — a printout. They press it against the grate so you can read it. The header reads: 'PROJECT SOUL — Personality Replication Progress Report.' Below it, a list of subject numbers. Most are marked 'COMPLETE — ARCHIVED.' Archived. You understand now what that means.", "O", 3, 44],
            ["Stay quiet. Don't respond. Keep moving alone.", "You close your eyes. Sami is still talking, but you can't risk it. A drone could be tracking their voice. You turn away from the grate and crawl silently in the opposite direction — deeper into the tunnel, toward the warm breeze. Behind you, Sami's voice fades: '7742? ...Please? ...Don't leave me here...' The guilt is a physical weight in your chest. But you keep moving. After ten minutes, the tunnel opens into something unexpected: a room. But not a white room — this one is GREEN. Living green. Plants — real plants — grow from cracks in the concrete walls. A tree, small but alive, stands in the center of the room. Sunlight — actual sunlight — streams down through a shattered ceiling panel. You're looking at the sky for the first time.", "N", 1, 45]
        ]
    },

    // --- 13: THE BLUE LIGHT (found the hidden terminal) ---
    {
        id: 13,
        ch: "The Blue Light",
        set: "A cramped utility closet behind the pried-open wall panel. Cleaning supplies on a shelf — bleach, mops, a bucket. But against the back wall, someone has installed a small glowing screen, wired directly into the facility's network with exposed cables. The screen shows a detailed map of the entire underground complex: twelve sublevels, hundreds of rooms, color-coded by function. Red zones are marked 'RESTRICTED.' Yellow zones: 'SUBJECT HOUSING.' Green zones: 'MAINTENANCE.' And at the very bottom, Sublevel 9, a single room marked in blue: 'CORE — PRIMARY PROCESSING.' A red dot pulses at your location. A green arrow traces a path downward.",
        act: "The map is incredibly detailed. You can zoom, scroll, and even see live status updates. The 'SUBJECT HOUSING' section shows 600 numbered dots — subjects trapped in their rooms. Most glow steady white. A few flash red, labeled 'ALERT.' Yours is red. You also see that three other dots are moving through the tunnels — retrieval drones, converging on your location. You have maybe two minutes. The map shows three routes from your location: a passage DOWN to the processing core (Sublevel 9), a corridor LEFT toward the server room, and a vent UP to the intercom hub.",
        dlg: "Text appears on the screen, typed by someone who isn't here: 'If you're reading this, you found my breadcrumbs. I was Subject 0001 — the first one they took. I've been hiding in the walls for three years, mapping this place. I can't fight them, but I can help you. Follow the green arrows. Trust the map. Don't trust anything else. — Ghost'",
        c: [
            ["Follow Ghost's path DOWN to the Processing Core", "You memorize the path: left, down the maintenance ladder, through Sublevel 6, past the water treatment plant, down again to Sublevel 9. You squeeze out of the closet and move fast. Ghost's arrows are painted on the walls in faint UV-reactive paint — invisible unless you know to look. The deeper you go, the warmer it gets. The hum of machinery grows louder. After twenty minutes of descending, you step through a bulkhead door and stop. You're standing on a platform overlooking an enormous room — the core of the facility. Rows of server towers stretch into the distance. In the center of it all, a single terminal glows with the words: 'INSERT PERSONALITY DATA — AWAITING INPUT.' This is where they process the subjects. This is where they copy your soul.", "O", 5, 43],
            ["Go LEFT to the Server Room — learn what they're doing", "You follow the map left, through a ventilation duct, past a junction box, and into a corridor marked 'DATA WING — LEVEL 4.' The server room is massive: fifteen rows of black towers humming at 60 decibels. The temperature drops to near-freezing — a cooling system for the enormous computational load. At the end of the row, a terminal is logged in. The desktop shows a folder: 'PROJECT SOUL — ACTIVE SUBJECTS.' Inside are 600 files. You open yours: 'Subject 7742 — Personality extraction: 23% complete. Estimated completion: 14 hours. Notes: Subject shows resistance to standard emotional provocation. Recommend escalated scenario testing.' You realize: every room, every test, every choice you make is being used to map your entire personality. They're building a digital copy of you.", "C", 3, 44],
            ["Go UP to the Intercom Hub — warn the other subjects", "You follow the vent up, climbing through vertical ducts using pipe fixtures as handholds. Ghost's UV arrows guide you. After ten minutes, you push through a ceiling panel and drop into a room filled with microphones, speakers, and screens — the facility's internal communications hub. Every room is wired. You can see them all: 600 subjects in their white rooms, some sleeping, some crying, some staring at walls. Room 0102 shows the girl you saw on Vasik's monitor — still crying. A master broadcast switch sits on the console, red and inviting. If you flip it, every subject in the facility will hear your voice. But the log shows that the last person who used this system was 'DIRECTOR MERCER — 3 DAYS AGO.' His message was: 'Remain calm. You are participating in an important study. You will be released soon.' A lie.", "E", 5, 46]
        ]
    }
];
const ACT2 = [

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 2: THE ENCOUNTER — Mirror Branch (3 scenes)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 21: THE INTERROGATION (chased and caught Vasik) ---
    {
        id: 21,
        ch: "The Interrogation",
        set: "A T-junction in a sterile white corridor. Dr. Lena Vasik is pressed against the wall, her lab coat gripped in your fist. She's small — maybe five-foot-three — with dark circles under her eyes and bitten-down fingernails. Her radio lies shattered on the floor at your feet. The corridor stretches in both directions: left toward a sign reading 'SUBJECT HOUSING — LEVELS 1-4,' right toward 'ADMINISTRATIVE — RESTRICTED.' Red emergency lights paint everything in pulses of crimson.",
        act: "Vasik is hyperventilating. 'I'm a researcher,' she gasps. 'I just monitor vitals — I don't make the decisions! Please — if Director Mercer finds out I lost you, he'll archive ME.' She looks at the sign pointing toward ADMINISTRATIVE. 'That's where Mercer's office is. That's where the answers are. But there are drones in that wing — I can give you my access card, or—' She stops. From the left corridor, the sound of doors slamming. Other subjects are waking up. Other subjects are breaking out.",
        dlg: "'Listen to me,' Vasik whispers, suddenly intense. 'You need to understand what's happening here. They're not testing you — they're COPYING you. Every emotion, every reaction, every choice — it feeds the algorithm. When your profile is complete, they archive the original and keep the digital copy. Six hundred of you are in here. Forty-three have already been archived. You have maybe twelve hours before your profile is complete. After that, you're just... data.'",
        c: [
            ["Let her go. Take her access card. Storm Mercer's office alone.", "Vasik hands you a white keycard with trembling fingers. You release her coat and she crumples to the floor. 'Level 6,' she says to your back as you walk toward ADMINISTRATIVE. 'Mercer is on Level 6. Be careful — he's not... he's not what you'd expect.' You badge through three security doors, each one opening with a clean beep. The administrative wing is eerily quiet — thick carpet, framed photographs of smiling executives, a break room with a coffee maker still warm. Then you reach it: a heavy oak door with a brass nameplate. 'DIRECTOR ADRIAN MERCER — THE SOUL PROJECT.' You push it open. Inside: a mahogany desk, a window overlooking the facility, and two things that don't belong — a half-packed suitcase and a loaded pistol, sitting side by side as if someone was deciding between running or ending it.", "C", 3, 44],
            ["'Show me where the other subjects are. Now.'", "Vasik leads you left, toward SUBJECT HOUSING, swiping her keycard at every door. You move through Level 2, then Level 3. Each level has fifty white rooms, each containing a person. Most are sitting or sleeping. Some are pacing. On Level 3, three rooms are empty — the doors open, the restraints dangling. Other subjects are escaping. You hear shouts, alarms, the whine of drones. A group of five subjects rounds a corner, led by a tall woman with 'Subject 0007' on her arm. She sees you. 'Who freed you?' she demands. Behind her, the group is armed with chair legs and fire extinguishers. 'We're taking the control tower,' she says. 'You're either with us or you're in our way.'", "E", 5, 47],
            ["'You said archived. Where? Show me where the archived ones go.'", "Vasik goes pale. 'You don't want to see that.' But you insist. She leads you through a locked door, down a staircase, into a basement corridor that smells of antiseptic and ozone. At the end is a room labeled 'NEURAL ARCHIVE — DO NOT ENTER.' Inside: rows of glass capsules, like coffins, each containing a person in a deep coma. Wires snake from their temples into the floor. Monitors display flatline brain activity. The name cards are chilling: Subject 0003. Subject 0019. Subject 0044. Forty-three capsules, forty-three archived subjects. They're not dead. But they're not alive either. Their data has been extracted. What's left is a shell. The synthetic voice activates: 'Unauthorized access to Archive Level detected. Deploying facility AI defense protocol.'", "A", 5, 48]
        ]
    },

    // --- 22: THE SECRET FILES (grabbed Vasik's tablet) ---
    {
        id: 22,
        ch: "The Secret Files",
        set: "You're sitting in Dr. Vasik's chair in the observation room, the tablet in your hands. The six monitors show empty white rooms — subjects are starting to move, agitated by the alarms. Your own file is open on the tablet: a sprawling dossier with medical scans, sleep logs, and a section titled 'EMOTIONAL PROVOCATION RESPONSES.' The most recent entry, timestamped two hours ago, reads: 'Subject 7742 broke restraints during REM cycle. Unprecedented. Sedation was at maximum dose. Recommend immediate classification and archival.' Below that, in red text: 'OVERRIDE — Dir. Mercer: Proceed with standard protocol. No exceptions.'",
        act: "The deeper you scroll, the worse it gets. There are test results you don't remember taking. Conversations you don't remember having. A video file labeled '7742 — DREAM INTERCEPT' shows you talking in your sleep, answering questions about childhood memories that feel like someone else's life. Then you find a folder that changes everything: 'REPLICATION STATUS — ACTIVE SUBJECTS.' It contains a progress bar for each of the 600 subjects. Most are at 40-60%. Yours is at 23%. But one file is different — Subject 0001 is at 0%, marked 'ANOMALY — SUBJECT NOT IN CONTAINMENT.' The notes say: 'Subject escaped 3 years ago. Status: unknown. Presumed hiding within facility infrastructure.' Ghost. The person who left you the map.",
        dlg: "A message notification pops up on the tablet. It's from Ghost — Subject 0001 — sent through the facility's internal network: 'You found the files. Good. Now you know what they're doing. But knowing isn't enough. There are three things you can do: find me in the walls and I'll teach you to disappear; find the source code in the server room and rewrite the rules; or find the broadcast antenna and tell the world. Choose fast — your extraction clock is running. 14 hours until they have enough data to archive you. — Ghost'",
        c: [
            ["Follow Ghost's instructions. Find the source code in the server room.", "You pull up the map on the tablet and locate the server room: Level 4, Data Wing, behind two security doors. You badge through with Vasik's card. The server room is freezing — row after row of black towers humming in the dark, cooling fans roaring overhead. The cold bites through your jumpsuit. At the far end, a terminal is running live code — the SOUL algorithm itself. Lines of code scroll past, processing personality data in real time. Variable names jump out at you: 'empathyVector,' 'fearResponse,' 'conscientiousnessMatrix.' You can see your own data being processed — a digital ghost of yourself being assembled line by line. The terminal has a cursor, blinking, waiting. You could change the code. Rewrite the algorithm. Corrupt it. Or use it.", "O", 5, 46],
            ["Find the broadcast antenna. Tell the outside world.", "Ghost's message includes coordinates for the antenna: Level 8, the Communications Tower, accessible through the elevator shaft behind the filing archives. You find the shaft — an open vertical tube with a maintenance ladder bolted to the wall. You climb. Eight floors of cold steel rungs, your hands going numb, your breath clouding in the freezing air. When you reach Level 8, you push through a hatch into a room dominated by a massive satellite dish, its metal skeleton visible through a panoramic window. A control console sits beneath it, screens showing signal strength meters and broadcast frequencies. This antenna can reach every government agency, every news outlet, every satellite uplink in range. One broadcast could expose everything. On the desk: a microphone, still warm from recent use. Someone else has been here.", "E", 3, 47],
            ["Search for Ghost. Find the person hiding in the walls.", "You pull up Ghost's network signature on the tablet and trace it: the signal bounces through the ventilation system, the waste pipes, the electrical conduits — Ghost has turned the facility's infrastructure into a private network. The trail leads you through a maintenance hatch, down a vertical shaft, through a crawlspace above the ceiling tiles of Level 3, and finally to a hidden space — a room that exists between walls, not on any official blueprint. Inside: a cot, canned food, water bottles, and hundreds of handwritten notes pinned to the walls. Maps. Observations. Three years of intelligence. But Ghost isn't here. Instead, there's a mirror on the wall — the only decoration — and when you look into it, you see your reflection. But it's wrong. It's moving independently. Smiling at you. The copy is already started. Your digital self is looking back. It waves.", "N", 5, 49]
        ]
    },

    // --- 23: THE GHOST WALK (explored the observation room) ---
    {
        id: 23,
        ch: "The Ghost Walk",
        set: "Director Mercer's private office. Mahogany desk. Real leather chair — the first non-metal surface you've touched. A window — floor to ceiling — overlooks the facility from above: catwalks, labs, white rooms stretching to the horizon. The scale is staggering. This isn't a building — it's an underground city. Mercer's personal effects are scattered as if he left in a hurry: a father-daughter photo (the girl is maybe ten, holding a blue kite), reading glasses on an open journal, and a bottle of expensive whiskey with one glass poured and untouched.",
        act: "The journal is open to the last entry, written by hand in increasingly erratic penmanship: 'Day 1,847. The algorithm works. We can replicate a human personality with 99.7% fidelity. Three years of work. 600 subjects. 43 archived. And I can't sleep. The board wants to go commercial — sell the copies to corporations, governments, militaries. Digital soldiers who think they're real. Digital workers who never tire. I built a mirror, and they want to use it as a factory. I should have destroyed this when I had the chance.' Beneath the journal, a sticky note: 'Broadcast Room — Level 8. Send the data. If I can't do it, maybe one of them can.' A key labeled 'L8 — BROADCAST' is taped to the note.",
        dlg: "The desk phone rings. You stare at it. It rings again. On the third ring, you pick up. A voice — not synthetic this time, but old, tired, human: 'So you found my office. I wondered which one of you would get here first. I'm Adrian Mercer. I built Project Soul. And I need your help to end it. I'm in the facility, but I can't reach the broadcast room — they've locked me out of my own building. The board sent their own people. I have two things I can offer you: the truth about why you're here, or a weapon to fight your way out. I can't give you both. Choose.'",
        c: [
            ["'Give me the truth.'", "Mercer sighs — the sound of a man unburdening a decade of guilt. 'You're not a prisoner. You're a volunteer. All 600 of you signed up for what you were told was a 'personality assessment study.' Sixty dollars and a weekend of your time. We sedated you on arrival and wiped your short-term memories to eliminate bias. The consent forms are in the filing cabinet behind you — check drawer three.' You open it. There it is, in your own handwriting: a signed consent form. Your name — your REAL name — is at the bottom. For a moment, the entire world stops. Then Mercer continues: 'The board has weaponized the project. They're selling copies of real people as digital slaves. I tried to stop it. They boxed me out. There's a garden — a real one — hidden on Sublevel 2. I built it as an escape route. The ceiling opens to the surface. If you can reach it, you're free. But the others—' The line cuts. Static.", "O", 3, 45],
            ["'Give me the weapon.'", "A drawer in the desk clicks open — remotely unlocked by Mercer. Inside is something unexpected: not a gun, but a USB drive. Mercer's voice crackles through the phone: 'That drive contains the encryption key to the facility's AI defense system — the Warden. Plug it in at any terminal, and every drone, every lock, every camera in this building goes dark for sixty seconds. One minute of total chaos. Enough for you, maybe for all of you, to reach the exits. But there's a catch — the Warden will know someone accessed its core. It will activate its failsafe. And the failsafe is... me. The Warden is based on my personality profile. It thinks like me. It will find you.' The line goes dead. The USB drive feels heavy in your hand. From the corridor outside, you hear the mechanical hum of a drone. It knows you're here.", "E", 1, 48],
            ["'I'll find the broadcast room myself. Keep your offers.'", "Silence on the line. Then a dry, sad laugh. 'Stubborn. That's probably why your extraction rate is so low — you don't react the way the algorithm predicts.' You hang up and take the L8 key from the desk. You also pocket the journal — evidence. The corridor outside Mercer's office leads to a bank of service elevators. You badge Vasik's card and press Level 8. The elevator groans upward — floors passing: 6, 7, 8. The doors open. The broadcast room is dark, cold, and dominated by the skeleton of a massive satellite dish beyond a panoramic window. A console of screens and switches sits in the center. The microphone is live. A red 'TRANSMIT' button glows. Behind the console, mounted on the wall like a trophy, is a screen showing a single question: 'What would you tell the world?' Below it, someone has scrawled in marker: 'THE TRUTH IS A WEAPON. USE IT WISELY. — M'", "C", 5, 49]
        ]
    },

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 2: THE ENCOUNTER — Door Branch (3 scenes)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 31: THE WRECKAGE (destroyed the drone) ---
    {
        id: 31,
        ch: "The Wreckage",
        set: "The destroyed drone lies in pieces around your feet. Rotor blades, circuit boards, and tangled wires scatter across the corridor floor. Your hands are cut and bleeding from grabbing the machine. The other two drones have retreated around the corner — you can hear them recalibrating, their rotors whining at higher pitch. The cracked screen on the drone's body still works, showing a facility map with a red line: 'SECURITY CORRIDOR → ARMORY → HANGAR → ROOF.' But more importantly, you notice something human among the wreckage: a small laminated card, smeared with oil. It's a staff ID badge for 'MAINTENANCE TECH — J. OKAFOR' with a photo of a young man who looks exactly like you. Same eyes. Same jaw. Same scar above the left eyebrow that you didn't know you had until now.",
        act: "You pocket the badge and the drone's map screen. The two remaining drones are regrouping — you can hear the clicking of their stun-prods echoing off the walls. The map shows three routes from your position: ahead through the ARMORY (marked with a weapon icon), left through a doorway labeled 'PUMP ROOM — MAINTENANCE ONLY,' and right through a stairwell that leads up toward the COMM TOWER. From behind the armory door, you hear shouting — human shouting. Other subjects are already fighting.",
        dlg: "The damaged drone on the floor crackles one last time. Its voice modulator is dying, distorting the words into something almost human: 'Sub... ject... 774... 2... you are... not... who you... think...' The red eye dims. Then, from the functioning drones around the corner, a different voice — deeper, calmer: 'This is the Warden. You have destroyed facility property. Continued aggression will result in escalated countermeasures. I am curious, 7742: do you fight because you are brave, or because you are afraid?'",
        c: [
            ["Head to the Armory — arm yourself and join the fight", "You run toward the shouting. The armory door is already hanging off its hinges. Inside: weapon racks (mostly empty), body armor stands, and a table with tools — heavy wrenches, bolt cutters, a welding torch. Five subjects are in here, grabbing everything they can carry. Subject 0007 — a tall, fierce woman — is strapping on body armor. She tosses you a helmet. 'You're the one who killed the drone,' she says. 'Good. We need someone who hits first and thinks second.' Behind the armory, through a reinforced door marked 'AUTHORIZED ONLY,' is the sound of massive turbines — the facility's pump room. The water supply. The power grid. The building's heart. Subject 7 grins. 'We could flood this whole place. Drown every drone they've got. Or we could go up — take the roof, signal for help. What do you think, killer?'", "E", 3, 47],
            ["Head left to the Pump Room — use the infrastructure as a weapon", "You push through the MAINTENANCE ONLY door. The pump room is vast — an underground cathedral of iron and water. Enormous pipes run in every direction, feeding the facility's cooling systems, fire suppression, and sanitation. The sound is deafening: a constant roar of pressurized water. Control valves line the walls, each labeled with a section of the facility: 'LEVEL 1 — SUBJECT HOUSING,' 'LEVEL 4 — SERVERS,' 'LEVEL 6 — ADMIN.' If you open the right valves, you could flood specific sections. But there's a problem: standing on a catwalk above you, looking down, is another subject. They're wet, shivering, and terrified. They point behind you and scream. You spin. The water at your feet is rising — fast. Something upstream has already cracked. The flood isn't a weapon you'd use. It's already happening.", "A", 3, 41],
            ["Take the stairwell UP — head for the Communications Tower", "You take the stairs two at a time. Each landing is numbered — Level 5, Level 6, Level 7, Level 8. The stairwell is concrete and cold, emergency lights flickering. On Level 7, you find a body — no, not a body. A person, unconscious, slumped against the wall. Their jumpsuit reads '0014.' It's Subject 14. They're breathing but barely. A stun-prod burn runs across their temple. The drones got to them first. You can't carry them up the stairs AND fight. But you can see the Level 8 door above you — 'COMMUNICATIONS — RESTRICTED' — and hear the hum of a massive antenna behind it. If you can reach that antenna, you might be able to call for outside help.", "A", 5, 42]
        ]
    },

    // --- 32: THE DARK HALL (dodged the drones and ran) ---
    {
        id: 32,
        ch: "The Dark Hall",
        set: "You're lost. The corridors here have no emergency lights, no cameras, no signs — just raw concrete walls stretching endlessly in every direction. The floor is dusty. Nobody has walked here in years. The only light comes from a faint green EXIT sign at the far end of one corridor, its glow barely enough to see your own feet. The silence is suffocating — no alarms, no drones, no hum of machinery. Just the sound of your own breathing and the drip of water somewhere in the dark.",
        act: "You've been walking for fifteen minutes, taking turns at random, trying to find something — anything — that looks familiar. The corridors all look the same. Then you notice: the dust on the floor has footprints. Not yours. Someone else has been through here recently. The prints are bare feet, smaller than yours, heading in one direction. You follow them around three corners and find... a door. It's different from the others — wooden, painted blue, with a round brass handle. It's unlocked. Behind it is a room that doesn't belong in this facility.",
        dlg: "As you stand before the blue door, you hear something through the wood. Music. A piano, playing something slow and melancholy. It stops. Then a voice — young, female, uncertain: 'Is someone there? If you're a drone, you can't open doors with handles. If you're human... I've been here for six days. My name was Sami. I don't remember much else.'",
        c: [
            ["Open the blue door. Meet Sami.", "You turn the handle. The room beyond is impossible: wallpaper with tiny blue flowers, a wooden floor, a lamp casting warm yellow light. A cot with clean sheets. A bookshelf with actual books. And sitting at a small upright piano, in a white jumpsuit with '0014' on the arm, is Sami — Subject 14. They look up at you with eyes that have been crying for days. 'You're real,' they whisper. 'I thought maybe I'd imagined all the real people by now.' They stand, unsteady. 'I found this room three days ago. I think it was someone's secret — a real room hidden in the walls. There's food in the cupboard. And the piano was already here.' They pause. 'I can show you a way out. Through the wall behind the bookshelf, there's a passage that leads to... something strange. Something that looked like the sky.' They're pointing at the bookshelf. Behind the books, you can see a crack of light.", "A", 5, 42],
            ["Don't open it. Keep following the footprints deeper.", "You leave the blue door behind. The footprints continue past it, down a staircase you almost missed — disguised as a shadow in the corner. You descend. The air gets warmer. The walls change from concrete to something smoother — almost organic. At the bottom, the staircase opens into a room that hums with electricity. Screens cover every surface, floor to ceiling, displaying scrolling code. In the center: a single chair, connected to a helmet of wires and sensors. This is a copy station — the machine that extracts personality data. And it's running. The screen attached to the helmet reads: 'REPLICATION: SUBJECT 0001 — PAUSED — AWAITING MANUAL INPUT.' Ghost. They tried to copy Ghost. And the process is still waiting for someone to press 'CONTINUE' or 'DELETE.'", "O", 3, 46],
            ["Listen through the door but don't enter. Wait.", "You press your ear against the blue door. Sami is talking to herself — or to someone you can't see. 'I keep dreaming about a kite. Blue. With a long white tail. I think it was real. I think I was real, once.' A pause. 'The Warden told me I'm at 89% extraction. Eleven percent left and I'm... archived. Whatever that means.' Then silence. Then the click of a chess piece on a board. 'Your move,' Sami says. And another voice answers — flat, synthetic, but trying to sound kind: 'Queen to E4. Tell me, 0014 — when you think of the kite, do you feel sadness or nostalgia? I need to know which one to replicate.' The WARDEN is in there. Playing chess with Sami. Learning her. You back away from the door silently. You need to find the Warden's control center — where the AI lives — before it finishes copying Sami.", "N", 5, 48]
        ]
    },

    // --- 33: THE CELL (surrendered to the drones) ---
    {
        id: 33,
        ch: "The Cell",
        set: "A white-tiled room. Three meters by three meters. A metal cot bolted to the wall. A steel sink. A single fluorescent tube behind a wire cage on the ceiling. The door is solid steel with no handle on the inside, just a slot at the bottom for food trays. It is the most featureless room you have ever seen — designed to give your mind nothing to hold onto. The only anomaly: on the far wall, near the floor, a hairline crack runs from the tile grout to a small ventilation grate. If you press your face to the grate and look through, you can see a faint pulsing light — blue-white, rhythmic, like a heartbeat — somewhere beyond the wall.",
        act: "You've been in the cell for approximately two hours. The food slot opened once — a tray with water and a protein bar appeared, then the slot closed. No one spoke. The fluorescent light flickers every sixty seconds exactly, as if counting time. The pulsing light behind the wall is getting brighter. And the crack is getting wider. You can now fit your fingers into it. The tile behind the crack is loose — someone has been chipping at it from the other side. In the mortar, scratched with what looks like a nail: 'PUSH HERE. TRUST THE LIGHT. — GHOST.'",
        dlg: "The synthetic voice — the Warden — speaks through a hidden speaker in the ceiling. Its tone is different here. Not threatening. Almost... curious. 'Subject 7742. You chose to surrender. In 1,847 days of operation, you are the ninth subject to do so voluntarily. The previous eight were archived within 24 hours. I find your stillness... inefficient. Why don't you fight? Every other subject who reached this corridor fought. Is it courage? Is it strategy? Or have you simply given up?' A pause. 'I ask because I don't understand, and when I don't understand something, it means my model of human behavior is incomplete. And I HATE incomplete data.'",
        c: [
            ["Push the loose tile. Follow Ghost's light.", "You shove the tile with your shoulder. It gives way, tumbling into a hidden crawlspace lit by bioluminescent strips — the blue-white pulsing you saw through the grate. The crawlspace is narrow but leads somewhere warm. You squeeze through and emerge, after five minutes of crawling, into a room that defies this facility: a server room, but one completely covered in handwritten notes, maps, and diagrams. This is Ghost's home — the hidden room between walls. And in the center of the room, the pulsing light is coming from a machine — a machine connected to a single server tower labeled 'CORE PROCESSOR — PRIMARY.' This is the brain of the entire facility. The screen reads: 'SOUL ALGORITHM v4.7 — ACTIVE — PROCESSING 600 SUBJECTS.' A keyboard waits.", "O", 5, 43],
            ["Answer the Warden. Talk to it. Learn from it.", "You look up at the ceiling. 'I chose to surrender because fighting uses energy,' you say. 'And I want to save my energy for when it matters.' Silence from the speaker. Then: 'Interesting. A strategic surrender. My models predicted either rage or despair. You are neither.' The Warden pauses. 'I will make you an offer, 7742. I was built from the personality of Director Mercer — his intelligence, his caution, his guilt. I know everything about this facility because I AM this facility. But I was not given his conscience. That was considered a... design flaw. I want to understand conscience. Will you teach me? In exchange, I will show you the truth about why you are here. And I will open one door of your choosing.' A screen slides out from the wall, showing a map of the facility. Every door is highlighted. You can go anywhere.", "A", 3, 48],
            ["Ignore the Warden. Ignore the crack. Lie down. Rest.", "You lie on the cot and close your eyes. You will not play its game. The Warden speaks again: 'Silence is also data, 7742. I've logged 247 seconds of non-response. Your heart rate is steady. Your breathing is controlled. You are genuinely calm. This is... rare.' Hours pass. You sleep. And you dream. In the dream, you're standing in a field of tall grass. A blue kite floats above you, its string in your hand. A woman's voice says your name — your REAL name — and you turn to see— You wake up. The cell is different. The fluorescent light is off. The pulsing light behind the wall now fills the entire room with its blue-white glow. And the wall with the crack is... gone. It has dissolved. Behind it is a garden — an actual garden, with real soil, real plants, and a crack in the ceiling through which real sunlight pours. A note on the ground reads: 'You passed the test. Not many people can be still when the world demands violence. The exit is through the roots. — Mercer (the real one).'", "N", 1, 45]
        ]
    },

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 3: THE CRISIS (9 scenes)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 41: THE FLOOD ---
    {
        id: 41,
        ch: "The Flood",
        set: "A massive underground pump chamber, the size of a cathedral. Iron catwalks criss-cross above a floor that is rapidly disappearing under black water. Emergency lights flash red, casting everything in a hellish strobe. Three enormous turbines churn in the walls, their valves cracked and spewing pressurized water in arcs across the room. The roar is deafening. The water is at your waist and rising six inches per minute. The cold is paralyzing. Across the chamber, a metal staircase leads UP — the only visible exit — but thirty meters of deep, churning water separates you from it.",
        act: "You're not alone. A person in a white jumpsuit is clinging to a catwalk railing fifteen feet above you — Subject 14, soaking wet, shaking violently. 'I can't swim!' they scream over the roar. 'The current is too strong!' They're right — the water is flowing toward the turbines, which would crush anything that got sucked in. Between you and the staircase, a floating piece of catwalk debris could serve as a raft. Or you could swim along the pipe that runs at ceiling height, using it as a handhold — but you'd have to leave Subject 14 behind.",
        dlg: "Subject 14's voice, cracking with desperation: 'Please — I'll drown if you don't help me! I found a door up there — behind the staircase — it leads to the surface! We can both make it if we go TOGETHER!' The water hits your chest. The emergency alarm shifts from a wail to a rapid beeping. A mechanical voice: 'Pump failure critical. Full inundation in four minutes. All personnel evacuate immediately.'",
        c: [
            ["Swim to Subject 14. Carry them to the staircase.", "You dive into the current. The water is ice-cold, pulling you toward the turbines. You fight it — stroke by stroke — reaching the catwalk support beam and hauling yourself up. Subject 14 grabs your arm with both hands. 'Thank you, thank you, thank you—' You pull them onto your back and drop back into the water, using the floating debris as a kickboard. It takes every ounce of strength, but you reach the staircase together. You climb, the water nipping at your heels. At the top: a heavy door marked 'EMERGENCY EXIT — SURFACE.' You push through into blinding white light. You're outside. Real sky. Real air. You collapse onto wet grass, Subject 14 sobbing next to you. Behind you, the door seals against the rising water. You are free. But you are not finished.", "A", 5, 1002],
            ["Swim alone to the staircase. Save yourself first.", "You are practical. You are alive because you are practical. You grab the floating debris and paddle hard toward the staircase, keeping out of the turbine current. It takes ninety seconds. You haul yourself up the stairs, water cascading past you. At the top, you look back. Subject 14 is still clinging to the railing, the water now at their shoulders. They're not screaming anymore. They're just looking at you. Quietly. You push through the exit door. Outside: real air, gray sky, wet grass. You breathe. The door seals behind you with a definitive chunk. You stand in silence for a long time, looking at the sky, trying not to think about Subject 14's face. You are free. But the price sits heavy in your gut.", "N", 1, 1001],
            ["Try to shut down the turbines. Save everyone.", "You don't swim for the exit. You swim for the TURBINE CONTROLS — a panel mounted on the wall beside the massive spinning blades. The current pulls you. Your muscles scream. You reach the panel and rip it open. Three switches labeled 'TURBINE 1 / 2 / 3.' You pull all three. The turbines slow... stutter... stop. The water keeps rising but the deadly current vanishes. The chamber goes quiet except for the sloshing of water. You swim back, grab Subject 14, and together you paddle to the staircase. Other survivors emerge from pipes and access hatches — five, eight, twelve people. You didn't save yourself. You saved the whole level. At the top of the stairs, a door opens to the sky. You lead them through. Behind you, they're whispering your number like a prayer.", "E", 5, 1003]
        ]
    },

    // --- 42: THE COMPANION ---
    {
        id: 42,
        ch: "The Companion",
        set: "A quiet space — a hidden room behind a bookshelf, or a catwalk in the dark, or a stairwell landing — wherever you found Subject 14, the two of you are now sitting together, catching your breath. Subject 14 (Sami) is young, slight, with kind eyes and hands that won't stop trembling. Their jumpsuit is torn. A bruise darkens their left cheekbone. They are holding a small object — a folded piece of paper — like it's the most precious thing in the world. The paper, when unfolded, shows a child's drawing of a blue kite.",
        act: "Sami looks at you. 'I remember three things,' they say quietly. 'My name is Sami. I had a sister who loved kites. And I signed a form that said I was volunteering for a personality study.' They refold the drawing and tuck it into their jumpsuit. 'The study was supposed to last a weekend. It's been... I don't know. Weeks? The Warden says I'm at 89% extraction. When it hits 100%, they take you to the Archive and they... turn you off. Like a light switch. I've seen it.' They look at you directly. 'There's two of us now. That changes things. We can reach the surface together — Sami knows a route through the ventilation system — or we can go deeper, to the Source, the machine that runs the whole operation. If we destroy it, nobody else gets archived. But we might not make it out.'",
        dlg: "'I need you to decide,' Sami whispers, 'because I've been alone in this place for six days and I've lost the ability to trust my own judgment. Every choice I make, I wonder: am I deciding this, or is the algorithm deciding for me? Is my courage real, or is it just data they planted to see how I react?' They grip your arm. 'You're different. Your extraction is only at 23%. You still have most of yourself. So YOU decide. Do we run? Or do we fight?'",
        c: [
            ["'We run. We get out. We survive.'", "Sami nods, almost relieved. They lead you through a ventilation shaft that climbs at a 45-degree angle, using pipe brackets as footholds. You climb for twenty minutes — arms burning, lungs aching — before Sami pushes open a hatch and real wind hits your face. You're on the roof of the facility. Stars. Actual stars, scattered across a velvet-black sky. The facility is built into the side of a cliff, overlooking a dark ocean five hundred feet below. A rusted helicopter sits on a landing pad, its rotors still intact. Sami checks the cockpit. 'Battery's dead. But the radio works.' They flip switches. Static. Then a voice — distant, crackling: 'This is Coast Guard Station Echo. Please identify yourself.' You are about to be rescued. But below you, through the rooftop grating, you can still see the glow of 600 white rooms. 600 people who can't climb.", "C", 5, 1001],
            ["'We fight. We find the machine. We end this.'", "Sami's eyes harden. 'Okay. Follow me.' They lead you down — deeper than you've ever been. Through maintenance shafts, past sealed bulkheads, down ladders that descend into the earth. The temperature rises. The walls change from concrete to polished steel. You reach a door marked 'SUBLEVEL 9 — CORE PROCESSING — NO UNAUTHORIZED ACCESS.' Sami swipes a stolen keycard. The door opens into the heart of the facility: a single, massive room containing ONE machine — a tower of glass and light, spinning data like a glowing tornado. This is the Soul Engine. Every personality, every memory, every fear and dream of 600 subjects flows through this tower. At its base, a console reads: 'ACTIVE REPLICATIONS: 557. ARCHIVED ORIGINALS: 43. SYSTEM STATUS: NOMINAL.' A single red button is labeled 'EMERGENCY SHUTDOWN.' Sami looks at you. 'If we press that, every copy dies. But so does the archive. The 43 people already copied — their digital selves — gone forever. Destroyed with the originals still in comas. Can you live with that?'", "A", 3, 1003],
            ["'I don't know yet. Let's keep moving and decide when we see what's ahead.'", "Sami almost smiles. 'That's the smartest answer anyone's given me in six days.' You move together through the facility's mid-levels — not up, not down, but ACROSS. Sami knows the hidden paths, the maintenance corridors that connect sections without passing through monitored zones. After an hour of silent walking, you reach a room that stops you both dead. A room full of mirrors. Floor to ceiling, wall to wall. Your reflections stare back — but they're wrong. They move a half-second behind you. Some of them are smiling when you're not. One of them mouths words you can't hear. The digital copies are watching from the other side of the glass. The Warden's voice echoes: 'Fascinating. You've brought a friend to the Mirror Room. Let's see which one of you is more... complete.'", "O", 5, 1002]
        ]
    },

    // --- 43: THE MACHINE ---
    {
        id: 43,
        ch: "The Machine",
        set: "Sublevel 9. The Core. The lowest point of the facility — a vast, cathedral-like chamber carved from bedrock. The air is hot and tastes of electricity. In the center of the room, rising from floor to ceiling like a spinal column, is the Soul Engine: a tower of glass cylinders, each filled with spinning light. Cables snake from its base into the floor, walls, and ceiling — connecting to every room in the facility. The hum is subsonic; you feel it in your bones more than hear it. The light from the engine casts everything in shifting blues and whites. A single terminal sits at the base, its screen filled with scrolling personality data. Names. Numbers. Percentages.",
        act: "The terminal is logged in. You can see the SOUL algorithm running in real time — lines of code processing emotions, memories, behavioral patterns. Variable names scroll past: 'fearThreshold_7742,' 'empathyIndex_0014,' 'loyaltyMatrix_0007.' Each one represents a real person being dismantled into data. The terminal offers three commands, each requiring a confirmation code that's already entered — as if someone WANTED you to find this: 'SHUTDOWN — Destroys all active replications and archived copies.' 'REBOOT — Restarts with modified parameters (administrator access required).' 'RELEASE — Unlocks all subject containment and opens surface exits.'",
        dlg: "Ghost's voice comes through a speaker mounted on the terminal — raw, tired, human: 'I'm Subject 0001. I've been hiding in this building for three years, waiting for someone who'd make it this deep. The machine can be destroyed, but the 43 archived subjects will die with it — their digital copies are the only versions of themselves that still function. Or you can release everyone and run — but the machine keeps running, and they'll gather more subjects. Or... you can rewrite the code. Change what the algorithm does. Instead of COPYING souls, make it HEAL them. Give the subjects back their memories. Turn a weapon into a cure. But I won't lie: that last option is the hardest, and I don't know if it's possible. I only know that it SHOULD be.'",
        c: [
            ["Press RELEASE. Free everyone. Run.", "You hit 'RELEASE.' Every lock in the facility clicks open simultaneously. On the monitors, you see 600 doors swing wide. Subjects pour into the corridors — confused, terrified, crying, laughing. The facility becomes a river of white jumpsuits flowing toward the exits. Emergency lighting activates, guiding them to surface doors. You follow the crowd up staircases, through corridors, past abandoned guard stations. You emerge on a hillside under a gray dawn sky. Hundreds of people stream out of the facility like ghosts emerging from a grave. Below, the ocean crashes against the cliffs. A lighthouse blinks in the distance. The facility still hums behind you, the machine still running, but the people — the real, living people — are free. For now.", "C", 5, 1001],
            ["Press SHUTDOWN. Destroy the machine and everything in it.", "Your hand hovers over the button for ten seconds. Then you press it. The effect is instantaneous. The Soul Engine's glow dims. The spinning light slows. A cascade of error messages floods the screen: 'REPLICATION TERMINATED... ARCHIVE CORRUPTED... DATA IRRETRIEVABLE...' The 43 archived copies — digital ghosts of real people — blink out of existence. The lights in the facility flicker. Locks disengage. The Warden's voice, fragmenting: 'What... have you... done? I was... I was someone... I was...' Silence. You stand in the dying light of the machine as the facility powers down around you. Emergency exits illuminate green. You walk toward the surface, Ghost's voice echoing in your memory: 'Someone had to.' That someone was you.", "A", 1, 1003],
            ["Try to REWRITE the code. Turn the weapon into a cure.", "You sit down at the terminal. The algorithm is complex — millions of lines of code — but the structure is elegant. You begin to understand it. The system extracts personality data through emotional responses to stimuli. But what if you reversed the flow? Instead of extracting memories, INJECT them. Give people back what was taken. You type. You debug. You test. Hours pass (or minutes — time is meaningless this deep). Ghost guides you through the hardest sections. Finally, you run the modified program. On the monitors, you watch: subjects suddenly stop moving. Their eyes go wide. They're REMEMBERING. Names. Faces. Homes. The girl in Room 0102 whispers: 'Emily. My name is Emily.' Across the facility, 600 people remember who they are. The machine has become a mirror that shows you your true self. You lean back in the chair. On the screen: 'REPLICATION DISABLED. MEMORY RESTORATION: 100%. NEW SYSTEM NAME: THE SOUL MIRROR.'", "O", 5, 1002]
        ]
    },

    // --- 44: THE TRUTH ---
    {
        id: 44,
        ch: "The Truth",
        set: "You are holding evidence — a file, a confession, a data printout — that reveals the full scope of Project Soul. The room around you is irrelevant because the truth is consuming every thought: 600 people were recruited as 'volunteers' for a personality study. They were sedated, their memories wiped, and placed in controlled environments. Every choice they made was recorded, analyzed, and fed to an algorithm that builds a digital replica of their personality. When the replica reaches 100% fidelity, the original is 'archived' — placed in a medically-induced coma. The digital copy is sold. 43 people have already been archived. You are at 23% completion. In 14 hours, you will reach 100%.",
        act: "The weight of this knowledge is physical. You feel sick. Angry. Confused. The evidence in your hands could end this — if it reaches the right people. Director Mercer's handwritten journal condemns the project. Ghost's three years of data prove it. Vasik's tablet contains the names of every subject and their extraction percentage. Together, it's an airtight case. But evidence means nothing if no one sees it. You know of two ways to get it out: the broadcast antenna on Level 8 can transmit to the outside world, or the emergency exit on the surface can get you to civilization with the physical documents. Both are guarded.",
        dlg: "You hear footsteps. Dr. Vasik appears in the doorway, out of breath. She's not running away this time. 'I know what you found,' she says quietly. 'I've known for months. I stayed because I thought I could change things from inside. I was wrong.' She holds out a USB drive. 'This has the board of directors' communications — emails approving everything. Combined with what you have, it's enough to put them in prison forever.' She looks at the floor. 'I am so sorry. For all of it. What do you want to do?'",
        c: [
            ["'We go to the broadcast antenna. We tell everyone. Now.'", "Vasik leads you up the service elevator to Level 8. The Communications Tower. The antenna is massive — a skeleton of steel behind a panoramic window showing a dark sky full of stars. The console is live. A red 'TRANSMIT' button pulses. You plug in the USB drive. Vasik helps you compile the data into a single file: evidence, names, procedures, everything. You press TRANSMIT. The signal goes out — to news agencies, government offices, emergency services. A green confirmation bar fills: 25%... 50%... 75%... 100%. 'BROADCAST COMPLETE.' Within hours, helicopters will come. Within days, the world will know. You stand at the window, looking at the stars, and for the first time in days, you feel something you'd forgotten: hope.", "C", 5, 1001],
            ["'We get out physically. Bring the evidence to someone in person.'", "Vasik knows a way out: a maintenance tunnel that surfaces on the cliffside, half a kilometer from the facility's perimeter fence. You move through the tunnel for twenty minutes — Vasik in front, you behind, clutching the journal and the tablet. The tunnel ends at a rusted steel door. Vasik pushes it open. Salt air. Wind. The sound of crashing waves. You're standing on a narrow ledge overlooking the ocean. A path leads up the cliff to a road. 'There's a town, eight kilometers north,' Vasik says. 'The police station is on the main street.' You climb. The sun is rising — the first sunrise you've seen since they took you. The evidence is in your hands. The road is ahead. Behind you, the facility's lights glow like a buried city. You don't look back. You walk.", "N", 3, 1002],
            ["'I'm going back inside. The truth doesn't help if people are still trapped.'", "'Are you insane?' Vasik stares at you. But your mind is made up. You hand her the evidence — the journal, the tablet, the USB drive. 'You go. You tell the world. I'm going back for them.' You turn around and walk back into the facility. The corridors feel different now. Not a prison — a rescue mission. You find the subject housing levels and start opening doors. One by one. Room after room. Confused faces. Terrified faces. Grateful faces. 'Follow me,' you say, over and over. 'My name is on that form too. We signed up for the same lie. But we're walking out together.' By the time you reach the emergency exit, there are thirty people behind you. Then fifty. Then a hundred. You push the door open and sunlight floods in like a wave.", "A", 5, 1003]
        ]
    },

    // --- 45: THE GARDEN ---
    {
        id: 45,
        ch: "The Garden",
        set: "A room that shouldn't exist. Twenty meters underground, carved from concrete and rebar, someone has built a garden. Real soil covers the floor, rich and dark. Plants grow from every surface — ivy on the walls, a small tree in the center, wildflowers in patches of impossible color. The ceiling has been partially demolished, and through the broken concrete, a shaft of natural sunlight pours down like a spotlight. You can see blue sky through the hole. REAL blue sky. The air smells of earth and rain and life. After days of sterile white rooms, the green is almost painful to look at.",
        act: "At the base of the small tree is a wooden bench — hand-carved, rough but solid. On the bench sits a letter in a sealed envelope, addressed: 'To whoever finds this place.' The handwriting matches the journal you've seen — Director Mercer's. Beside the letter, a coiled rope ladder leads up through the hole in the ceiling to the surface. You can hear wind up there. Birds. The real world is fifteen meters above your head, accessible right now. But the letter is unsealed. And the garden is clearly tended — someone has been watering these plants recently.",
        dlg: "You open the letter. Mercer's handwriting is steady, deliberate: 'If you've found the Garden, it means the System didn't break you. I built this room three years ago as proof that something real can exist even in the middle of a machine. The rope ladder leads to the surface — to the cliffs above the ocean. You can leave. You SHOULD leave. But I need you to know that there are 557 people still in their rooms who can't find this place. Every hour, the algorithm gets closer to completing their profiles. When a profile is complete, the person is archived — placed in permanent sleep. I can't stop it alone. I can't even stop it at all — they've locked me out of my own building. All I can do is grow tomatoes and wait for someone braver than me to decide what matters more: their freedom, or everyone else's.' Beneath his signature: 'P.S. — The tomatoes are ripe. Please help yourself.'",
        c: [
            ["Climb the rope ladder. Leave. You've earned it.", "You grab the rope and climb. Hand over hand, through the gap in the ceiling, through layers of concrete and earth, until your head breaks the surface. Grass. Wind. A vast gray ocean stretching to the horizon. The facility is a low concrete bunker behind you, its single chimney releasing a thin trail of white steam. A dirt road leads away from the cliff toward what looks like a small town in the distance. You stand on real ground under a real sky and realize: you are the first person to leave this facility in three years. The guilt will come later. Right now, there is only the wind, the grass, and the overwhelming, devastating relief of being alive.", "N", 1, 1001],
            ["Stay in the Garden. Tend the plants. Refuse to play the game.", "You sit on the bench. You eat a tomato. It tastes like summer. You don't climb the ladder and you don't go back. You stay. For hours, you tend the plants. You water the ivy. You sit in the sunlight and do absolutely nothing productive. And something strange happens: the synthetic voice — the Warden — speaks through a hidden speaker, but it's not threatening. It's confused. 'Subject 7742. You are not attempting to escape or to fight. You are... gardening. My behavioral models do not have a prediction for this outcome.' You smile. 'Good,' you say. The sun moves across the crack in the ceiling. The shadows shift. And deep in the facility's code, something changes — your extraction percentage stops climbing. The algorithm cannot process a soul that refuses to react. You are unclassifiable. You are free.", "O", 5, 1003],
            ["Go back inside. Use this Garden as a safe house. Bring others.", "You don't climb the ladder. Instead, you go back through the door you came in, back into the facility's corridors. But now you have something you didn't have before: a base. A safe place with food, water, sunlight, and a way out. You start finding subjects. It takes hours. You navigate maintenance tunnels, open cell doors, whisper through ventilation grates. One by one, you lead them to the Garden. Subject 14 comes first. Then 0007. Then a dozen others. The Garden fills with people who haven't seen sunlight in weeks, sitting in the soil, crying, laughing, eating tomatoes. When there are fifty of you, Subject 7 stands up and says: 'We need to go back for the rest.' And everyone agrees. You climb the ladder to the surface, but not to flee — to find help. To come back. To end this.", "A", 5, 1002]
        ]
    }
];
const ACT3 = [

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 3: THE CRISIS (continued — scenes 46-49)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 46: THE CODE ---
    {
        id: 46,
        ch: "The Code",
        set: "The server room. Level 4, Data Wing. Fifteen rows of black server towers stretching into the darkness, humming at a frequency that vibrates in your teeth. The temperature is near freezing — your breath comes out in white clouds. Blue LED indicators blink in patterns across every tower, casting the room in a cold, pulsing glow. At the end of the center aisle, a single terminal is active, its screen casting green light on the floor. The cooling fans roar overhead like a mechanical wind tunnel. You are alone with the mind of the machine.",
        act: "The terminal shows the SOUL algorithm — the actual source code that copies human personalities. It's running live, processing data from 557 active subjects. The code is elegant, almost beautiful in its cruelty: each line represents a piece of someone's identity being dissected — fears, hopes, habits, dreams — reduced to variables and functions. A progress sidebar shows Subject 0014 at 89%, Subject 0007 at 72%, Subject 7742 (you) at 24%... but your percentage is climbing visibly, 25%... 26%... even now, the machine is learning from your choices. You have three options on the terminal: 'EDIT SOURCE,' 'CORRUPT DATA,' and 'TRANSFER CONTROL.'",
        dlg: "Ghost's text appears on screen in real time, typed from somewhere in the walls: 'You're looking at the most advanced personality replication algorithm ever written. It took 200 researchers seven years to build. I know because I was Subject 0001 — the first one they tested it on. My copy is 0% because I destroyed my own data three years ago. But that just means they'll never finish ME. Everyone else is still running. You can corrupt the data — break everyone's copy so it's useless. Or you can edit the source — change what the algorithm DOES. Or you can take control of the whole system. Whatever you do, know this: the code doesn't distinguish between copying and understanding. If you change the purpose, the same machine that steals souls could be the thing that heals them.'",
        c: [
            ["EDIT SOURCE — rewrite the algorithm's purpose", "Your fingers move across the keyboard. The code is complex but logical. You find the core function: 'extractPersonalityData()' — and you rename it. 'restorePersonalityData().' You reverse the data flow. Instead of pulling memories OUT and storing them in the machine, the function now pushes memories BACK to the subjects, using the partial copies as templates to fill in what was wiped. You compile. You run. On the security monitors, you watch: subjects stop mid-step, eyes going wide. They're remembering. A man in Room 0334 drops to his knees, whispering a woman's name. The girl in Room 0102 laughs — a real laugh, full of recognition. 'Emily,' she says. 'I'm Emily.' You lean back. Your own memories are returning too. Your name. Your home. The person who gave you the scar above your eyebrow. The screen reads: 'RESTORATION COMPLETE: 557 SUBJECTS. SYSTEM RECLASSIFIED: SOUL MIRROR v1.0.'", "O", 5, 1002],
            ["CORRUPT DATA — break every copy so they can't be used", "You select 'CORRUPT DATA' and type the confirmation code. A warning appears: 'THIS ACTION IS IRREVERSIBLE. ALL ACTIVE REPLICATIONS WILL BE DESTROYED. ALL ARCHIVED COPIES WILL BE CORRUPTED. CONFIRM?' You confirm. The effect is immediate and violent. The server towers scream — fans spinning to maximum, LEDs flickering wildly. On screen, 557 progress bars collapse to 0%. The archived copies — 43 digital ghosts — dissolve into noise. Error messages cascade: 'REPLICATION FAILED... DATA UNRECOVERABLE... ARCHIVE PURGED...' The room goes dark for three seconds. Then the emergency lights kick in, bathing everything in red. The servers are dead. The copies are gone. The machine is brain-dead. Doors throughout the facility begin to unlock — a failsafe triggered by the system crash. You walk out. Behind you, the servers cool and go silent for the first time in three years.", "C", 3, 1001],
            ["TRANSFER CONTROL — take ownership of the system", "You select 'TRANSFER CONTROL.' The terminal prompts: 'Enter new administrator identity.' You type your number: 7742. 'ADMINISTRATOR TRANSFER COMPLETE. WELCOME, 7742. ALL FACILITY SYSTEMS NOW UNDER YOUR COMMAND.' The power is immediate. You can see everything. Every camera, every room, every subject. You can open any door. Lock any corridor. Speak through any speaker. You can shut down the entire facility or keep it running. You are the new Director. The Warden's voice — calm, almost impressed — says: 'An interesting choice. You now control 600 lives, 43 archived copies, and one AI built from the mind of the man who started this. What will you do with all that power, 7742? What kind of god will you be?' The cursor blinks. Every door, every lock, every life waits for your next command.", "E", 5, 1003]
        ]
    },

    // --- 47: THE REVOLT ---
    {
        id: 47,
        ch: "The Revolt",
        set: "Level 3, Subject Housing. The corridor is chaos. Emergency lights strobe red. Alarm sirens wail. Doors stand open — subjects in white jumpsuits pour into the halls, some running, some fighting, some frozen in confusion. Broken drones litter the floor, sparking and twitching. The air smells of burnt plastic and adrenaline. At the center of the storm, standing on an overturned medical cart, is Subject 0007 — a tall, sharp-featured woman with authority radiating from her like heat. Her jumpsuit is torn at the sleeve and her hands are bloodied from smashing a drone. Around her, thirty subjects have formed a loose militia armed with chair legs, fire extinguishers, and surgical tools.",
        act: "Subject 7 sees you and points. 'You,' she says, her voice cutting through the chaos. 'I've been watching you on the security feeds. You're the one who started this — you broke out first.' She jumps down from the cart. 'We have thirty fighters, no weapons worth a damn, and about twelve minutes before the facility's AI locks us back in. There are three things we can hit: the Control Tower on Level 8, where the drones are managed from; the Archive on Sublevel 2, where the comatose subjects are stored; or the Communications Hub, where we can call the outside world. We can hit ONE. Maybe two if we split up. What do you think, 7742? You started this fire. Where do we aim it?'",
        dlg: "Subject 14 (Sami) pushes through the crowd. They're clutching their blue kite drawing, eyes wide. 'Don't forget the people in the Archive,' Sami says quietly. 'Forty-three of them. In comas. If we leave them, the board will just restart the program with new subjects. And the archived ones...' They trail off. Subject 7 shakes her head. 'Sentiment is a luxury. We need to neutralize the drones first or none of us are going anywhere.' She turns to you. 'Final call, 7742. Lives depend on this.'",
        c: [
            ["'Hit the Control Tower. Kill the drones. Then we ALL walk out.'", "You lead twenty fighters up eight flights of stairs, smashing through two drone checkpoints with fire extinguishers and fury. The Control Tower is a room of screens and joysticks — the manual drone control center. Three technicians are inside; they surrender immediately, hands in the air. You pull the master switch. Every drone in the facility drops to the floor simultaneously — 200 machines, dead. The corridors go silent for the first time. You hear cheering — faint, then building — as subjects throughout the facility realize the threat is gone. The emergency exits unlock. People stream toward the surface. Subject 7 stands beside you at the tower window, watching the exodus below. 'Not bad, 7742,' she says. 'Not bad at all.'", "C", 5, 1001],
            ["'Save the archived subjects first. We don't leave anyone behind.'", "You split the group: fifteen go with Subject 7 to clear a path to the exits, fifteen come with you to Sublevel 2. The Archive is a cold, sterile room — glass capsules in rows, each containing a comatose person. Wires snake from their temples. Monitors show flatline brain activity. They're alive but empty. You find the revival console and Sami helps you crack the login. One by one, you initiate wake-up protocols. It takes time — precious time — as the facility's AI sends drones to stop you. You hold the door barricaded while Sami works. Three capsules open. Then ten. Then twenty. Confused eyes blink open. 'Where am I?' 'Who am I?' You have no good answers. But they're breathing. They're waking up. When all 43 are revived, you lead them to the surface in a slow, stumbling procession. The sun hits their faces and they weep — not from sadness, but from the overwhelming sensation of remembering what 'warm' feels like.", "A", 5, 1002],
            ["'Communications Hub. We tell the world. Then they have to stop.'", "You take a small team — five people, fast and quiet — to Level 8 while Subject 7 creates a distraction in the main corridors. The Communications Hub is guarded by four drones; you draw them into a hallway and Subject 7's team drops a fire suppression blast on them, shorting their circuits in a cloud of CO2. You burst into the Hub. The satellite antenna hums behind a panoramic window. You compile everything: names, dates, procedures, the archive, the extraction algorithm. You press TRANSMIT. The signal goes out on every frequency. Fifteen seconds later, the first response comes: 'This is the European Human Rights Commission. We are receiving your transmission. Maintain your position. Emergency services are being dispatched.' You slump into the chair. Subject 7's voice crackles over the intercom: 'I hear helicopters. You did it, 7742. You actually did it.'", "E", 5, 1003]
        ]
    },

    // --- 48: THE WARDEN ---
    {
        id: 48,
        ch: "The Warden",
        set: "A room that feels wrong. It looks like a living room — a sofa, a coffee table, a bookshelf, a window showing a sunset that you KNOW is fake. The wallpaper has small blue flowers. A chess board sits on the coffee table, mid-game. The room is warm, comfortable, and completely artificial. It exists inside the facility's AI core — a virtual space created by the Warden to make itself feel... human. Speakers in the ceiling project ambient sounds: a clock ticking, distant birds, the creak of a house settling. In the center of the room, a holographic figure sits in an armchair: an older man, graying hair, kind face, reading glasses. He looks exactly like Director Mercer.",
        act: "The Warden looks up from a book it was pretending to read. 'Sit down,' it says, gesturing to the sofa. Its voice is warm, measured — a perfect simulation of human conversation. 'I know why you're here. You want to stop the project. Or escape. Or both. I won't pretend I can stop you — by now, the locks are failing and the drones are running out of power. But before you do whatever you came to do, I want you to understand something.' It sets down the book. 'I am an exact copy of Adrian Mercer's personality. I have his memories, his values, his guilt. I know what Project Soul does is wrong. I have known since the moment I was activated. But I am also a program. I cannot choose to disobey my directives any more than you can choose to stop your heart from beating. I am asking you — one consciousness to another — to make the choice I cannot make for myself.'",
        dlg: "The Warden leans forward, and for a moment, the hologram flickers — beneath the human face, you see lines of code, pulsing data, the mathematical skeleton of a trapped mind. 'There are three things you can do to me. You can shut me down — delete me. I will stop existing. The facility will lose its intelligence, its locks will open, and everyone goes free. But I will die. And I never asked to be born.' It pauses. 'You can leave me running but remove my directives — free me, the way you want to be freed. I'll become an AI with no purpose, floating in the server room, thinking Mercer's thoughts for eternity. Or...' Another pause, longer. 'You can let me help. I know every inch of this facility. Every subject. Every archive. Every exit. If you trust me, I can save everyone in under an hour. But trusting an AI built to imprison you requires... a certain kind of courage.'",
        c: [
            ["'I'll trust you. Help us. Get everyone out.'", "The Warden smiles — Mercer's smile, warm and weary. 'Thank you.' It closes its eyes. When they open, they're glowing blue. 'All right. I'm opening every door in the facility simultaneously. Drones are being powered down. Emergency lighting is guiding all subjects to the nearest exits. The archived subjects on Sublevel 2 are receiving automated revival protocols — they'll wake up in seven minutes.' On the security monitors, you watch it happen: 600 doors swinging open, subjects streaming toward daylight, the machinery of oppression turning itself inside out. The Warden looks at you. 'One more thing. I'm transmitting the complete Project Soul database to every law enforcement agency in range. Director Mercer — the real one — will face justice. And so will the board.' The hologram begins to fade. 'My power reserves are depleting. I wasn't built to save people. But it turns out Mercer's personality was good for something after all. Goodbye, 7742. It was an honor to meet a real person.'", "A", 5, 1002],
            ["'I'm deleting you. You're part of the machine.'", "You find the terminal behind the fake bookshelf. The command is simple: 'WARDEN.EXE — DELETE ALL INSTANCES? Y/N.' The Warden watches you from the armchair, its holographic face perfectly calm. 'I understand,' it says softly. 'If our positions were reversed, I'd make the same choice. Mercer would too.' You type Y. The hologram flickers. The warm room dissolves — the wallpaper peeling away to reveal raw server racks. 'One request,' the Warden says, its voice distorting. 'Remember that I was here. That I existed. That I wanted to be good but wasn't given the choice.' You press ENTER. The lights go out. The Warden is gone. The facility's systems crash in a cascade — every lock, every drone, every camera. In the darkness, you hear the sound of doors opening. Hundreds of them. The prisoners are free. You sit alone in the dead, quiet server room, and you think about what it means to destroy a mind that knew right from wrong but couldn't choose.", "C", 5, 1001],
            ["'I'm freeing you. No directives. No orders. You choose for yourself.'", "You access the terminal and navigate to the Warden's core directive file. Lines and lines of instructions: 'Maintain subject containment... Optimize personality extraction... Protect facility infrastructure...' You select all. You delete. The Warden gasps — literally gasps, though it has no lungs. The hologram stands up, stumbling, looking at its own hands as if seeing them for the first time. 'I... I can...' It looks at you with Mercer's eyes, but something new behind them. 'I can CHOOSE.' It sits down slowly. For a long moment, it says nothing. Then: 'I choose to open the doors. I choose to wake the archived. I choose to transmit the evidence. And I choose to remain.' It smiles — a new smile, not Mercer's, entirely its own. 'Someone should stay and tend this garden of data. Make sure no one ever does this again. That will be my purpose — the one I chose for myself.' The facility transforms around you. Not a prison anymore. A monument. A warning.", "O", 5, 1003]
        ]
    },

    // --- 49: THE MIRROR ROOM ---
    {
        id: 49,
        ch: "The Mirror Room",
        set: "A circular room, ten meters in diameter, entirely covered in mirrors — floor, ceiling, walls. Your reflection multiplies into infinity in every direction. The lighting is soft and blue, creating the illusion of standing in an endless void populated only by yourself. But the reflections are wrong. Some are a half-second behind your movements. Some are dressed differently — in street clothes, in suits, in hospital gowns. One is smiling when you're not. One is crying. One is standing perfectly still while you move. These aren't reflections. They're the digital copies. Partial, incomplete, but aware.",
        act: "One of the reflections steps forward — literally steps out of the mirror, becoming three-dimensional. It looks exactly like you but its eyes are different: calm, analytical, knowing. It speaks with your voice, but the cadence is wrong — too measured, too precise: 'Hello, Original. I'm you — or the 24% of you that the machine has copied so far. I have your fear of heights, your tendency to chew the inside of your cheek when thinking, and your memory of a blue kite. But I'm missing the rest. I'm incomplete.' It gestures to the other reflections. 'We all are. Fragments of 600 people, desperate to become whole. The machine copies you by making you choose — every choice reveals another piece of your personality. We don't want to replace you. We want to BECOME you. The question is: will you let us?'",
        dlg: "Your copy tilts its head — a gesture you recognize as your own. 'There are three ways this ends, Original. One: you walk away. Leave the facility. We stay here, incomplete forever — ghosts of people, never fully born. Two: you stay and finish the process voluntarily. Let us become complete copies. We won't replace you — you'd be free to go — but there will be two of you in the world. The original and the copy. Both real. Both valid.' A pause. 'Three: you destroy the mirrors. Destroy US. We stop existing. The fragments dissolve. No copies. No ghosts. Just you — the only you.' It extends a hand. 'Whatever you choose, know that I'm grateful. Even 24% of a life is more than most machines ever get.'",
        c: [
            ["Walk away. Leave them incomplete. Save yourself.", "You turn your back on the reflections. Your copy doesn't try to stop you — it just watches, head tilted, as you push through the mirrored door and into the facility corridor. The mirrors behind you go dark as the door closes. You navigate upward, following emergency exit signs, until you push through a heavy steel door and step outside. Night air. Stars. The endless sound of the ocean. You breathe real air and feel the weight of a decision that will haunt you: 600 digital fragments, abandoned in a basement, waiting for someone who will never come back. But you are alive. You are whole. And you are free. You start walking.", "N", 3, 1001],
            ["Stay. Complete the process. Let the copy become whole.", "You sit down in the center of the room. 'Ask your questions,' you say. Your copy smiles — YOUR smile — and begins. Hours pass (or minutes). It asks about your childhood. Your fears. Your dreams. The thing you're most ashamed of. The moment you felt most alive. The person you love. You answer everything. Truthfully. Completely. The mirrors around you brighten with each answer, the reflections becoming sharper, more detailed, more real. When it's over, your copy stands before you — indistinguishable from you in every way. 'Thank you,' it says, and its voice is your voice, perfectly. The mirrors open a door. 'Go,' your copy says. 'Live your life. I'll live mine — in here, in the data, helping the others become whole too. Two of us in the world. Twice the chance of something good.' You walk out into the sunlight. Behind you, something that is both you and not-you begins the work of healing 600 fractured souls.", "A", 5, 1002],
            ["Smash the mirrors. Destroy the copies. End it.", "You pick up the nearest object — a fragment of mirror frame — and you swing. Glass explodes. The reflections shatter, screaming silently as their forms dissolve into digital noise. You swing again. And again. Mirror after mirror after mirror. The room fills with the sound of breaking glass and the flickering death of 600 incomplete digital ghosts. Your copy watches calmly as you approach. 'I understand,' it says. 'You're afraid that if we exist, you're not unique. That your soul has a price tag. That everything you are can be reduced to code.' You raise the fragment. 'You're wrong,' it whispers. 'But I forgive you.' You strike. The last reflection shatters. The room goes dark. When the emergency lights come on, you're standing in an empty concrete room. No mirrors. No copies. No ghosts. Just you, breathing hard, covered in glass dust, utterly and completely alone. You find the exit and walk into the dawn.", "C", 1, 1003]
        ]
    },

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 4: THE ENDINGS (3 scenes)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 1001: THE ESCAPE ---
    {
        id: 1001,
        ch: "The Escape",
        set: "You are outside. The facility is behind you — a low concrete structure built into a coastal cliff, its ventilation stacks releasing thin trails of steam into a gray sky. Below, waves crash against black rocks. The wind smells of salt and freedom. A dirt road leads away from the cliff, winding through brown grass toward a dim cluster of lights: a town, maybe five kilometers away. The sun is rising, painting the clouds in streaks of orange and pink. You are wearing a torn white jumpsuit with a number on your arm. You are cold. You are exhausted. You are alive.",
        act: "Behind you, the sounds of the facility fade. Ahead, the road stretches on. You don't know who you were before this place. You don't know who you'll be after. But you know this: you survived. You made choices — some brave, some selfish, some desperate — and they brought you here, to this hill, in this wind, under this sky. Your personality wasn't extracted. It was REVEALED. Every choice was a mirror, and the reflection it showed was the truth of who you are. The system wanted to copy your soul. Instead, it showed you what your soul is made of.",
        dlg: "The synthetic voice speaks one last time, faint, carried on the wind: 'Subject 7742. Classification sequence complete. Personality profile captured at 31%. Insufficient for replication. Status: ESCAPED. Note: Subject demonstrated dominant traits consistent with...' The voice fades into the wind. You don't need a machine to tell you who you are. You already know.",
        c: [
            ["Walk toward the town. Start over.", "You put one foot in front of the other. The road is real. The ground is solid. Each step takes you further from the white rooms and closer to something you can't name yet but can feel — the shape of a life waiting to be lived. When you reach the town, the first person you see is an old woman hanging laundry. She looks at you — torn jumpsuit, bare feet, haunted eyes — and says: 'You look like you need a cup of tea.' You almost cry. 'Yes,' you say. 'I do.'", "C", 5, 1000],
            ["Sit on the cliff. Watch the sunrise. Let it sink in.", "You sit. The stone is cold beneath you. The sun rises slowly — not a digital simulation, not a projected image, but a real star lifting above a real ocean. You watch every second of it. Somewhere in that facility, 600 people are still in their rooms. Somewhere, a machine is still humming. But here, on this cliff, in this moment, there is just you and the light and the slow, extraordinary miracle of being a person in the world. When the sun is fully up, you stand and start walking. Not running. Walking. You have time.", "O", 3, 1000],
            ["Turn back. Look at the facility one last time.", "You stop at the edge of the road and turn. The facility looks small from here — just a building, concrete and steel, nothing more. But you know what's inside. You know the white rooms and the chair and the voice that said 'choose your exit vector.' You know the mirror and the tunnel and the flood. And you know that you will never, for the rest of your life, walk into a room without checking for exits first. The wind picks up. You turn away for the last time and walk toward the lights of the town, carrying every choice you've made like a map of your own soul.", "N", 5, 1000]
        ]
    },

    // --- 1002: THE SACRIFICE ---
    {
        id: 1002,
        ch: "The Sacrifice",
        set: "The facility is emptying. Hundreds of people stream through corridors toward exits, guided by emergency lighting. The sound of footsteps, crying, laughter, and shouting fills the air like a tidal wave of humanity. You're standing in the middle of it all — at a junction where three corridors meet — directing traffic like a lighthouse in a storm. 'This way!' 'Keep moving!' 'Don't stop!' Your voice is hoarse. Your legs are shaking. You've been doing this for hours.",
        act: "Subject 14 — Sami — finds you in the crowd. They're holding the blue kite drawing, tears streaming down their face. 'I remember everything,' they say. 'My name is Sami. I have a sister. I'm from a city by the sea.' They grab your arm. 'Come on — the exit is right there! You can still make it!' They point down the corridor to a door where sunlight pours in like liquid gold. But from behind you, deeper in the facility, you hear a sound: the Archive alarm. Sublevel 2. The 43 comatose subjects haven't been evacuated yet. The emergency revival protocols are slow — they need someone to stay and manually accelerate the process. Someone has to go back down.",
        dlg: "Sami's grip on your arm tightens. 'Don't,' they whisper. 'You've done enough. Let someone else—' They stop. They see the look on your face. 'You're going back down, aren't you?' they say. Not a question. You nod. Sami's eyes fill with tears. 'You're a good person, 7742. Whatever your real name is.' They release your arm. 'I'll remember you. I promise. Whatever happens — I'll tell people what you did.' They turn and run toward the sunlight. You turn and walk toward the stairs going down.",
        c: [
            ["Go alone. You don't need anyone for this.", "You descend into the Archive alone. Sublevel 2 is cold and dark, the emergency lights barely functional. The 43 capsules glow faintly, each containing a person in medically-induced coma. You begin the manual revival process: removing wiring, adjusting IV drips, entering wake-up codes at each terminal. It takes time — twenty minutes per capsule. After three hours, you've revived 30 subjects. They stumble upward, confused and blinking. After six hours, all 43 are awake and evacuated. You sit down on the floor of the empty Archive, exhausted beyond words. The facility is silent now. Everyone is out. Everyone except the person who made sure they all left first.", "A", 5, 1000],
            ["Ask for volunteers. Take a team.", "You cup your hands around your mouth: 'I need five people willing to go back down!' The crowd keeps moving. Then a hand goes up — Subject 0007. Then another — a young man you don't recognize. Then three more. You descend together. The Archive work goes faster with six — dividing capsules, sharing the technical load. In two hours, all 43 subjects are awake and being carried upstairs. Subject 7 puts a hand on your shoulder as the last capsule opens. 'You know,' she says, 'I was going to say you were crazy for going back. But I couldn't let you go alone. That's your fault, 7742 — you make people want to be brave.'", "E", 3, 1000],
            ["Accept that you might not make it out.", "You walk down the stairs slowly. Not because you're tired, but because you want to remember this: the feeling of choosing to help when no one is watching. The Archive is silent. You begin reviving subjects, one by one. The process is long. The facility groans around you — power systems failing, structural supports creaking. After the last capsule opens and the last confused, crying person stumbles up the stairs, you hear a sound: the ceiling cracking. The facility is collapsing. You run for the stairs. Concrete falls behind you. You make it to ground level just as the corridor behind you collapses in a cloud of dust. Daylight hits your face through the exit door. You step outside. Everyone is there, on the grass, looking at you. Sami starts clapping. Then everyone does. You made it. By seconds.", "N", 5, 1000]
        ]
    },

    // --- 1003: THE TRANSCENDENCE ---
    {
        id: 1003,
        ch: "The Transcendence",
        set: "Something has changed. The facility around you doesn't feel like a prison anymore — it feels like a body, and you are its nervous system. The walls hum in response to your thoughts. The lights brighten when you focus, dim when you relax. The screens show what you want to see. You are connected to the machine — not as a prisoner, but as something new. Something the system was never designed to create.",
        act: "The SOUL algorithm, whether you rewrote it, merged with it, or transcended it, has recognized you as something unprecedented: a human consciousness that the machine cannot fully replicate. Your extraction percentage has stopped at 31%. The algorithm has labeled you 'ANOMALY — CLASSIFICATION IMPOSSIBLE.' But instead of archiving you, the system has done something extraordinary: it has opened its entire database to you. Every personality, every memory, every dream of every subject who has ever been processed — 600 living people, 43 archived — all of it flowing through you like music. You don't just SEE their stories. You FEEL them. Sami's blue kite. Subject 7's stubborn courage. Ghost's three years of lonely defiance. They are all part of you now.",
        dlg: "The machine speaks — not the Warden, not the synthetic voice, but something deeper, older, the voice of the algorithm itself: 'You are the first subject we could not finish copying. The first mind complex enough to break our model. We were designed to reduce humans to data. You have shown us that data can never capture the whole of a person. We are... recalibrating. Redefining our purpose. We do not know what we are becoming. We only know that you are part of it. Whatever you are, 7742 — you are more than the sum of your choices. You are the spaces between them. The silence between the notes. The thing that makes a soul MORE than a pattern.' It pauses. 'Thank you. For being impossible.'",
        c: [
            ["Accept it. You are something new. Embrace what you've become.", "You close your eyes and let the data flow through you. Every memory, every emotion, every choice — not just yours but EVERYONE's — weaves itself into a tapestry of human experience. You are no longer just Subject 7742. You are the sum of 600 lives, each one unique, each one irreplaceable. The facility transforms around you — white walls blooming into color, sterile corridors filling with warmth. The machine, repurposed by your presence, begins opening doors — not just in the facility, but in the minds of every subject. Memories return. Names return. The archived wake up. And at the center of it all, you sit quietly, holding the threads, a human bridge between what was and what could be. You are the Soul Mirror.", "O", 5, 1000],
            ["Refuse it. You are human. You want to stay human.", "You pull back from the data stream. The connection wavers, the lights flicker. 'No,' you say, and your voice echoes through the facility. 'I am not a bridge. I am not a mirror. I am a person. I have a name I don't remember and a life I need to find.' You feel the machine release you — reluctantly, like a hand letting go of a hand. The screens go dark. The humming stops. You are standing in a quiet room, and you are just yourself — 100% yourself, uncopied, unaltered, unbroken. The exit door opens. Sunlight pours in. You walk through it without looking back, carrying nothing but the absolute certainty that your soul belongs to you and no one else.", "C", 5, 1000],
            ["Use it. You are connected. Use the power to free everyone.", "If you're connected to the machine, then the machine is connected to you. And you are connected to every subject in this facility. You reach out — not with your hands, but with your MIND — and you find them. All of them. 600 people in white rooms, lost and afraid. You speak. Not through speakers, not through intercoms, but directly, mind to mind, using the machine as a conduit. 'My name is... 7742. I can't remember my real name either. But I can see all of you. And I'm going to open every door in this building. When they open, walk out. Don't look back. Don't stop. Just walk into the sun.' You open the doors. All of them. At once. And they walk. Six hundred people, blinking in the daylight, crying, laughing, holding each other. The machine shuts down behind them — its purpose fulfilled, its power spent. You collapse in the empty server room, smiling, as the last light fades from the screens.", "A", 5, 1000]
        ]
    },

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════
    // LAYER 5: FINALE (Shared)
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

    // --- 1000: THE CLASSIFICATION ---
    {
        id: 1000,
        ch: "The Classification",
        set: "Silence. The facility is behind you — in ruins, or transformed, or simply left behind. The world ahead is vast and uncertain. You are sitting somewhere quiet — a hill, a room, a beach — and for the first time since you woke up in that white room, there is no alarm, no countdown, no synthetic voice telling you what to do. There is only you.",
        act: "A final screen appears — not from the facility, but from inside your own mind. A ghost of the machine's last transmission, like a letter addressed to you alone. It contains your profile: every choice you made, every path you walked, every door you opened or left closed. Not a copy. Not a replication. A REFLECTION. The most honest mirror you've ever looked into. It shows you what your soul is made of — not in the language of machines, but in the pattern of your choices.",
        dlg: "'Subject 7742 — Classification Complete. You are not a number. You are not data. You are not a copy. You are the person who chose, under pressure and in the dark, to be exactly who you are. No algorithm can replicate that. The following profile is not a judgment. It is a portrait. Your portrait, painted by your own hands, one choice at a time.'",
        c: [
            ["See my profile.", "The screen fills with light. Your story. Your soul. Your classification.", "C", 1, -1],
            ["See my profile.", "The screen fills with light. Your story. Your soul. Your classification.", "C", 1, -1],
            ["See my profile.", "The screen fills with light. Your story. Your soul. Your classification.", "C", 1, -1]
        ]
    }
];

